\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 / \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2: No Devaluation Plans \b0\par\par\uc2 202 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 1996\par 18:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Suranyi denied any plans for a devaluation and said the government would stay on course with its present plans. The government has said previously it would maintain the monthly 1.2% crawling peg devaluations into the first quarter of 1997. \par \par \uc2  The special 8% surcharge on imports was announced in March 1995 to help narrow the country's deficit in the current account. The surcharge is being gradually phased out and is scheduled for total elimination next July. \par \par \uc2  The crawling peg devaluation was also introduced in March 1995 to stop speculation against the forint and provide an additional price advantage for Hungarian exports. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains a 4.5% \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band around the forint based on its daily fixings against the dollar. The forint typically trades near its strongest point against the dollar on the band. \par \par \uc2  On the open market the forint was quoted in late Friday trading at 152.12-152.17 against the dollar, or 2.17 percentage points below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Friday fixing of 155.19 forints to the dollar, according to the Hungarian Credit Bank in Budapest. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 06-09-96 \par \par \uc2  1611GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds96077tz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
