\par\par \b \uc2  HUNGARY FORINT WEAKENS, 85 PIPS BELOW BID. \b0\par\par\uc2 150 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Nov 30 (Reuter) - Hungary's forint opened weaker on Thursday as traders continued to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  The Hungarian unit opened at 135.10/15, almost half a forint weaker than Wednesday's close of 134.65/70 and around 85 pips weaker than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid of 134.30. \par \par \uc2  Traders gave several reasons for the continued buying of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some cited the end-of-the-month closing of December positions on the futures markets while others suggested that a number of forint-holding customers were acquiring hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay off loans. \par \par \uc2  "The maturing of some treasury bills is also creating demand," said Andras Szekely of Magyar Hitel Bank. \par \par \uc2  "We are approaching the time when the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to start thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Szekely added. \par \par \uc2  -- Duncan Shiels, Budapest newsroom (361) 266 2410 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040130drbu000xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
